Design Notes for USIU First-Year Companion

Overall Approach
The design of the USIU First-Year Companion was guided by three main goals:
Clarity – first-year students should be able to navigate and find useful information without confusion.
Consistency – visual elements such as colors, buttons, and cards remain consistent across sections.
Accessibility – both light mode and dark mode were implemented to support different preferences and environments.

Layout Choices
Header: Contains the project title and a tagline to set context. Positioned at the top for immediate recognition.
Card-Based Sections: Each section such as study tips, commuting info, and the estimator is displayed inside a card with shadows and rounded corners. This improves readability and makes the content feel organized.
Footer: Provides a clean finish with minimal text to avoid clutter.

Color and Theme
Primary Accent: A shade of blue (hex code 3a86ff) was chosen as it reflects trust, calmness, and professionalism.
Light Mode: Uses a clean white background with dark text for readability during the day.
Dark Mode: Switches background to near-black with light text to reduce eye strain at night.
Theme Toggle: Implemented so users can switch instantly, supporting different environments.

Typography
Font: System UI fonts such as Segoe UI, Roboto, and Ubuntu were used for readability across devices.
Headings: Larger, accent-colored headings create hierarchy.
Body Text: Regular, legible size with balanced line spacing for easy scanning.

Estimator Tool
Inputs: Students can enter commuting details and other cost-related values.
Output: Provides an estimated monthly cost to help students plan finances.
Reasoning: Many first-year students struggle with budgeting, so including a tool adds practical value beyond static information.

Wireframes
Home Page Wireframe: Shows header, tagline, and three main cards stacked vertically.
Estimator Wireframe: Dedicated card with form fields and calculate button.
Theme Toggle Wireframe: Small button in the header for switching modes.

Design Trade-offs
A minimalist design was chosen over heavy visuals to keep load times fast and make the site usable on slow internet connections.
Only essential interactive elements were added to balance simplicity and functionality.